.Dd September 29, 2026
.Dt KLDLOAD-BACKUP-RESTORE 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm kldload-backup-restore
.Nd receive a stream made by kldload-backup-pack into a new dataset tree
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Fl -host Ar host
.Op Fl -file Ar name | Fl -latest
.Op Fl -src-dir Ar dir
.Op Fl -target Ar dataset
.Nm
.Fl h | -help
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
reads a stream written by
.Xr kldload-backup-pack 8
from
.Ar host
over
.Xr ssh 1 ,
decompresses it and receives it into this machine's root pool, under a
dataset of its own:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
rpool/restore/NAME/<the sender's dataset, without its pool>
.Ed
.Pp
where
.Ar NAME
is the file's name without
.Ql .zfs.zst .
The live system is never touched, and nothing received is mounted: the
restored tree is there to be looked at, copied from, or promoted by hand.
.Pp
Without
.Fl -file
or
.Fl -latest
it lists the streams on
.Ar host ,
newest first, and asks which.
.Pp
A target that exists is refused, so a restore never overwrites.
If the receive fails, what it had made is removed again and the run can be
repeated.
.Pp
A stream sent raw, as every encrypted source is, arrives encrypted with
the sender's key, as its own encryption root; its facts file says
.Ql send_raw=1 .
To read it,
.Ql zfs load-key
it with the sender's passphrase or key, then mount it.
A stream from an unencrypted source lands unencrypted, even in an
encrypted pool.
.Pp
Everything the run prints is also appended to
.Pa /var/log/kldload/backup-restore.log .
It must run as root.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -host Ar host
Where the streams are.
Required, unless
.Ev BACKUP_REMOTE_HOST
is set.
.It Fl -file Ar name
The stream to restore: a file name in the directory, ending in
.Ql .zfs.zst ,
not a path.
.It Fl -latest
The newest stream in the directory.
.It Fl -src-dir Ar dir
The directory on
.Ar host ;
default
.Pa /var/lib/kldload-backups .
.It Fl -target Ar dataset
Receive under
.Ar dataset
instead of
.Pa rpool/restore/ Ns Ar NAME .
It must not exist.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print the usage and exit 0.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev BACKUP_REMOTE_HOST , BACKUP_REMOTE_DIR , BACKUP_SSH_OPTS
As for
.Xr kldload-backup-pack 8 .
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /var/log/kldload/backup-restore.log
Every run's output.
.It Pa rpool/restore/
Where restored trees are received.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width 1n -compact
.It 0
Received.
.It 1
Not root,
.Ar host
or the file unreachable, the target exists, or the receive failed.
.It 2
A usage error, or an invalid answer to the list.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Printed on a kldload desktop with an encrypted pool on 2026-09-29,
restoring the streams made in the examples of
.Xr kldload-backup-pack 8 .
.Xr ssh 1
was replaced by a stand-in that runs the remote command on the same
machine, so
.Ql backup1
is this host.
The restored datasets were destroyed afterwards.
.Ss The newest (--latest)
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kldload-backup-restore --host backup1 --src-dir /var/tmp/bptest-3308726-out --latest
[2026-09-29 23:18:05] [restore] using pool: rpool
[2026-09-29 23:18:05] [restore] verifying ssh access to backup1...
[2026-09-29 23:18:05] [restore] --latest → onyx-20260930T061103Z.zfs.zst
[2026-09-29 23:18:05] [restore] metadata for this backup:
    source_host=onyx
    source_dataset=rpool/bptest-3308726
    source_snapshot=backup-20260930T061103Z
    created_utc=20260930T061103Z
    created_local=2026-09-29T23:11:04-07:00
    stream_bytes_compressed=16742
    stream_bytes_estimated_raw=72928
    send_raw=1
    kldload_edition=free
    kldload_profile=desktop
    kernel=7.2.2-300.fc44.x86_64
[2026-09-29 23:18:05] [restore] restoring → rpool/restore/onyx-20260930T061103Z/<sender's tree>
[2026-09-29 23:18:05] [restore] creating rpool/restore/onyx-20260930T061103Z
[2026-09-29 23:18:05] [restore] streaming + decompressing + receiving (this is the slow part)...
[2026-09-29 23:18:05] [restore] DONE — restored tree rooted at: rpool/restore/onyx-20260930T061103Z

Next steps (operator):
  1) Inspect: zfs list -r rpool/restore/onyx-20260930T061103Z
  2) Nothing is mounted (-u). Pick a dataset and give it a mountpoint:
       zfs set mountpoint=/mnt/restored <dataset>
     It came raw, still encrypted: first
       zfs load-key <its encryption root>
     with the SENDER's passphrase or key.
  3) To PROMOTE back to live root, you must reboot into the live USB
     recovery shell and rename/swap the datasets. This script will
     not do that — too easy to brick a running system.
# (exited 0)
.Ed
.Ss A named stream (--file)
The command the pack printed, as it printed it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kldload-backup-restore --host backup1 --src-dir /var/tmp/bptest-3308726-out --file onyx-20260930T061850Z.zfs.zst
\&...
# (exited 0)
.Ed
.Ss Opening a raw restore
On a pair of throwaway pools with a key file, where the key was at hand:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# zfs get -H -o name,value encryption,encryptionroot,keystatus kldenc-3322473/restore/raw/secret
kldenc-3322473/restore/raw/secret	aes-256-gcm
kldenc-3322473/restore/raw/secret	kldenc-3322473/restore/raw/secret
kldenc-3322473/restore/raw/secret	unavailable
# zfs load-key -L file:///var/tmp/kldpool-3322473/key kldenc-3322473/restore/raw/secret
# zfs set mountpoint=/var/tmp/kldpool-3322473/rx kldenc-3322473/restore/raw/secret
# cat /var/tmp/kldpool-3322473/rx/f.txt
secret
.Ed
.Ss When it cannot
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kldload-backup-restore --host backup1 --src-dir /var/tmp/bptest-3308726-out --file onyx-20260930T061103Z.zfs.zst
\&...
[2026-09-29 23:18:12] [restore] FATAL: rpool/restore/onyx-20260930T061103Z already exists — refusing to overwrite.
[2026-09-29 23:18:12] [restore]   remove it first: zfs destroy -r rpool/restore/onyx-20260930T061103Z
# (exited 1)
# kldload-backup-restore --host backup1 --src-dir /var/tmp/bptest-3308726-out --file onyx-truncated.zfs.zst
\&...
[2026-09-29 23:18:12] [restore] creating rpool/restore/onyx-truncated
[2026-09-29 23:18:12] [restore] streaming + decompressing + receiving (this is the slow part)...
/*stdin*\e : Read error (39) : premature end
cannot receive: failed to read from stream
[2026-09-29 23:18:12] [restore] FATAL: recv pipeline failed
[2026-09-29 23:18:12] [restore] removed the partial rpool/restore/onyx-truncated
# (exited 1)
# kldload-backup-restore --host backup1 --src-dir /var/tmp/bptest-3308726-out --file ../etc/shadow.zfs.zst
kldload-backup-restore: --file is a NAME.zfs.zst in the directory, not a path: '../etc/shadow.zfs.zst'
# (exited 2)
# kldload-backup-restore --latest
kldload-backup-restore: no source: give --host HOST (or BACKUP_REMOTE_HOST)
# (exited 2)
.Ed
.Pp
.Ql onyx-truncated.zfs.zst
was the first 8000 bytes of a good stream.
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
.Bl -diag
.It "[restore] FATAL: cannot reach HOST:DIR"
.It "[restore] FATAL: no .zfs.zst files in HOST:DIR"
.It "[restore] FATAL: HOST:PATH not found"
.It "[restore] FATAL: TARGET already exists \(em refusing to overwrite."
Destroy it, or give another
.Fl -target .
.It "[restore] FATAL: recv pipeline failed"
ZFS's or
.Xr zstd 1 Ns 's
reason is above; the partial target has been removed.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ssh 1 ,
.Xr zstd 1 ,
.Xr kldload-backup-pack 8 ,
.Xr zfs-load-key 8 ,
.Xr zfs-receive 8
.Sh HISTORY
On 2026-09-29, writing this page: every restore had failed with
.Dq specified fs does not exist ,
because the target was never created, and after that failure the run hung
for good in
.Xr pv 1 ,
which does not exit when SIGPIPE is ignored; the host defaulted to
.Ql fiend ;
and the file name reached a remote shell unchecked.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An kldload Aq Mt anthony@kldload.com
.Sh CAVEATS
Promoting a restored root back to being the running system is not done
here: it means booting something else first.
.Pp
Received datasets keep the sender's mountpoints, unmounted; setting one
that another dataset uses would cover it.
